(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["correct",{"l:correct/how-do-you-state-exactly-what-a-function-must-do":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>You cannot prove or test \"correct\" until you have written down what correct means. A \u003cb>contract\u003c/b> has two parts: the \u003cb>precondition\u003c/b> (what the caller guarantees: sorted input, n &gt;= 1) and the \u003cb>postcondition\u003c/b> (what the function guarantees on return, stated as a relation between input and output). Write the postcondition as a small \u003cb>checker\u003c/b> function; checking an answer is usually much easier than computing it, and the checker becomes your first test.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A contract with a builder says what you get, not how the bricks are laid: \"three bedrooms, passes inspection\". The inspector does not rebuild the house; they check it against the list. A specification is that list for a function. It also says what \u003ci>you\u003c/i> owe: if you give the builder the wrong plot (a precondition broken), the result is not their fault.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Preconditions and postconditions\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Precondition:\u003c/b> what must be true of the arguments for the function to promise anything. \"a is sorted ascending.\" If the caller breaks it, the bug is in the caller, and the function may raise or return nonsense.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Postcondition:\u003c/b> what is true when the function returns, as a relation between inputs and result. \"0 &lt;= i &lt;= len(a), every item of a[:i] is &lt; x, every item of a[i:] is &gt;= x.\"\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>What is not said is free.\u003c/b> If the spec says \"the first k slots hold the kept values, in any order\", then order is not part of the answer, and a test that compares with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> is testing the wrong thing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Python's own documentation states \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> this way: the returned insertion point \u003ci>partitions\u003c/i> the list so that everything to its left is less than x and everything from it onwards is at least x. That sentence is a postcondition you can execute.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Checking is easier than solving\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Task\u003c/th>\u003cth>Solving costs\u003c/th>\u003cth>Checking an answer costs\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sort a list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n), with a subtle algorithm\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) for \"ordered\" plus O(n) with a Counter for \"same items\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Insertion point in a sorted list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n), easy to get off by one\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n): two \u003ccode>all(...)\u003c/code> scans\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Remove every val in place, any order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) with two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n): compare counts of the kept items\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shortest path length\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003ctd>still hard: needs a second algorithm\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When a checker is cheap, write it before the solution. When many answers are valid (any valid order, any peak, any one of several optimal schedules), a checker is the \u003ci>only\u003c/i> correct way to test.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Contracts in code\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Put the contract in the docstring, and while developing, check it automatically with a small decorator (the template). For in-place functions the postcondition must compare the arguments \u003cb>before\u003c/b> the call with the arguments \u003cb>after\u003c/b> it, so the decorator keeps a deep copy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A checker usually costs O(n) or O(n log n) per call, so wrapping every call can turn an O(log n) function into an O(n) one. That is fine in tests and wrong in production: switch the checks off once the tests pass (the template's \u003ccode>CHECK\u003c/code> flag, or run Python with \u003ccode>-O\u003c/code>, which strips \u003ccode>assert\u003c/code> statements). Deep-copying arguments costs O(size of the input) per call as well.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Before coding any non-trivial function, and always when the output allows several valid answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the statement is vague: writing the postcondition exposes every undecided case (ties, empty input, output order).\u003c/li>\u003cli>In tests: the checker replaces \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> against a single expected answer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Not every property is cheap to check. If verifying an answer is as hard as finding it (is this path really the shortest?), compare with a brute force instead, which is the stress-testing card's subject.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Checking candidate answers for the insertion point of x = 3 in a = [1, 3, 3, 7] against the postcondition \"a[:i] &lt; x &lt;= a[i:]\":\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Candidate i = 1: left part [1] is all &lt; 3, right part [3, 3, 7] is all &gt;= 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      1 | 3  3  7\n\u003cmark>valid: the first index where a[i] &gt;= 3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Candidate i = 2: left part [1, 3] contains 3, which is not &lt; 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      1  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> | 3  7\ninvalid: breaks \"everything left of i is &lt; x\"\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Candidate i = 0: right part [1, 3, 3, 7] contains 1, which is not &gt;= 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    | \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  3  3  7\ninvalid: breaks \"everything from i on is &gt;= x\"\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Candidate i = 4: left part [1, 3, 3, 7] contains 3 and 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      1  \u003cmark>3  3  7\u003c/mark> |\ninvalid; the checker says why, which == against \"1\" would not\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import copy\nimport functools\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nCHECK = True     # switch off once the tests pass\n\n\ndef contract(pre=None, post=None):\n    \"\"\"Check pre(*args) before the call and post(old_args, new_args, result) after it.\"\"\"\n    def wrap(fn):\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        def checked(*args):\n            if not CHECK:\n                return fn(*args)\n            if pre is not None:\n                assert pre(*args), f\"precondition failed: {fn.__name__}{args!r}\"\n            old = copy.deepcopy(args)               # in-place functions change args\n            result = fn(*args)\n            if post is not None:\n                assert post(old, args, result), f\"postcondition failed: {fn.__name__}{old!r} -&gt; {result!r}\"\n            return result\n        return checked\n    return wrap\n\n\ndef is_sorted(a):\n    return all(p &lt;= q for p, q in zip(a, a[1:]))\n\n\ndef sorts_ok(old, new, result):\n    \"\"\"A sort's postcondition: ordered, and the same items with the same counts.\"\"\"\n    return is_sorted(result) and Counter(result) == Counter(old[0])\n\n\ndef insertion_point_ok(old, new, i):\n    \"\"\"bisect_left's postcondition: a[:i] &lt; x &lt;= a[i:].\"\"\"\n    a, x = old\n    return 0 &lt;= i &lt;= len(a) and all(v &lt; x for v in a[:i]) and all(v &gt;= x for v in a[i:])\n\n\n@contract(pre=lambda a, x: is_sorted(a), post=insertion_point_ok)\ndef insertion_point(a, x):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(a)\n    while lo &lt; hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if a[mid] &lt; x:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n\n\n@contract(post=sorts_ok)\ndef my_sort(a):\n    return sorted(a)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Specifying the algorithm instead of the result.\u003c/b> \"Uses two pointers\" is a design, not a postcondition; \"the first k slots hold the kept values\" is.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> when several answers are valid.\u003c/b> Use the checker; otherwise correct solutions fail your tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the old input.\u003c/b> A postcondition such as \"the same items as before\" needs a copy taken before an in-place call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A weak postcondition.\u003c/b> \"The output is sorted\" alone is satisfied by \u003ccode>return []\u003c/code>; a sort must also return a permutation of its input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shipping the checks.\u003c/b> O(n) checks around an O(log n) function in production defeat the point; turn them off, as SQLite does with its asserts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which postcondition does \u003ccode>def bad_sort(a): return []\u003c/code> satisfy, and which property catches it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It satisfies \"the output is ordered\" (an empty list is ordered). It fails \"the output is a permutation of the input\", for example \u003ccode>Counter([]) != Counter([2, 1])\u003c/code>. A sort needs both properties.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write the postcondition for \"remove every occurrence of val from nums in place and return k\", where the order of the kept values does not matter.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With \u003ccode>old\u003c/code> the list before the call and \u003ccode>new\u003c/code> the list after it: k equals the number of items of \u003ccode>old\u003c/code> different from val, and \u003ccode>Counter(new[:k]) == Counter(x for x in old if x != val)\u003c/code>. Nothing is promised about \u003ccode>new[k:]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The precondition says a is sorted, and a caller passes [3, 1, 2]. Whose bug is it, and what should \u003ccode>insertion_point\u003c/code> do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The caller's: the contract promises nothing for unsorted input. During development, the \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> check should fail loudly so the caller is fixed; in production the function may return any index, which is why the precondition is written down.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>27 Remove Element\u003c/b>: write the postcondition as a checker first (the Q above), then the solution, and test it with the checker rather than \u003ccode>==\u003c/code>. Expected: nums = [4, 1, 4, 2, 4], val = 4 gives k = 2 with {1, 2} in the first two slots in either order; nums = [] gives k = 0; O(n) time, O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>58 Length of Last Word\u003c/b>: write the contract in your own words: what is a word, what happens with trailing spaces, and what the constraints guarantee (at least one word). Expected: \"  fly to   the moon  \" gives 4; \"a\" gives 1; an oracle for tests is \u003ccode>len(s.split()[-1])\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to write a contract with pre- and postconditions and turn it into a checker that tests answers even when many are valid.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/how-do-you-prove-a-loop-is-correct-and-that-it-s":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>loop invariant\u003c/b> is a statement about the loop's variables that is true before every iteration. Prove three things: it holds before the first iteration (\u003cb>initialise\u003c/b>), one iteration keeps it true (\u003cb>maintain\u003c/b>), and when the loop exits, the invariant plus the exit condition gives the answer (\u003cb>use\u003c/b>). Add a \u003cb>variant\u003c/b>, a non-negative integer that strictly decreases each iteration, and the loop must stop. Write ranges as half-open [lo, hi), and most off-by-one errors disappear.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Climbing a ladder: if you can reach the first rung, and from any rung you can reach the next, you can reach them all. That is induction, and an invariant is induction applied to a loop. The variant is the number of rungs left: it goes down by at least one per step and cannot go below zero, so the climb ends.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Initialise, maintain, use: the running maximum\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def running_max(a):\n    \"\"\"Largest item of a non-empty list.\"\"\"\n    best = a[0]\n    for i in range(1, len(a)):\n        # invariant: best == max(a[:i])\n        if a[i] &gt; best:\n            best = a[i]\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Initialise:\u003c/b> before the first iteration i = 1 and best = a[0] = max(a[:1]).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Maintain:\u003c/b> if best = max(a[:i]), then after comparing with a[i], best = max(a[:i + 1]).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use:\u003c/b> the loop exits with i = len(a), so best = max(a[:len(a)]) = max(a).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Variant:\u003c/b> len(a) - i falls by one per iteration and never drops below zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Insertion sort\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The invariant of the outer loop: \u003ci>before iteration i, a[:i] holds the original first i items, in sorted order.\u003c/i> Each iteration takes x = a[i], shifts the larger items of the sorted prefix one slot right, and drops x into the gap, so a[:i + 1] is sorted: maintained. At exit i = n, so the whole list is sorted. The inner loop has its own variant: j falls by one per step and stops at 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finding the invariant is the creative part. A good recipe: write the postcondition, then \u003cb>weaken it with the loop variable\u003c/b>. \"best is the max of a\" becomes \"best is the max of a[:i]\"; \"a is sorted\" becomes \"a[:i] is sorted\".\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Half-open ranges\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Write every range as [lo, hi): lo is included, hi is not. Then:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>the length is hi - lo, and the range is empty exactly when lo == hi;\u003c/li>\u003cli>adjacent ranges share a boundary: [lo, mid) and [mid, hi) cover [lo, hi) with no gap and no overlap;\u003c/li>\u003cli>it matches \u003ccode>range(lo, hi)\u003c/code>, slicing \u003ccode>a[lo:hi]\u003c/code> and C++ iterators, so you never translate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Dijkstra's EWD831 makes the case in one page: with an inclusive upper bound, the empty range starting at 0 would need an upper bound of -1, which is not a natural number. Many off-by-one errors are \u003cb>fencepost errors\u003c/b>: a 10-metre fence with a post every metre needs 11 posts. With half-open ranges, n items have n + 1 boundaries and n - 1 gaps, and the number of pages of size k for n items is \u003ccode>(n + k - 1) // k\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Termination in searches\u003c/h5>\u003cp>In a search over the candidate range [lo, hi), the variant is the range size hi - lo. Every iteration must make it strictly smaller; an update such as \u003ccode>lo = mid\u003c/code> when mid can equal lo is the classic infinite loop. The template's \u003ccode>floor_sqrt\u003c/code> keeps \"the answer is in [lo, hi)\" and loops while the range holds more than one candidate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Proving a loop costs a few sentences, and the invariant often \u003ci>is\u003c/i> the cost argument: the variant bounds the number of iterations (len(a) - i gives n - 1 for the running maximum; hi - lo halving gives about log2 n for a search). Insertion sort's inner loop runs at most i times, so the total is at most 1 + 2 + ... + (n - 1) = n(n - 1)/2 shifts: O(n^2) in the worst case (reversed input) and O(n) on sorted input, where the inner loop never runs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Any loop with more than one moving index, any in-place rearrangement, any search: write the invariant as a comment \u003ci>before\u003c/i> the loop body.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When an off-by-one error keeps coming back: the invariant tells you whether \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mid\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>mid + 1\u003c/code> is right.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When a loop sometimes hangs: find the variant; if you cannot, there may be an iteration where nothing shrinks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Simple \u003ccode>for x in xs\u003c/code> loops with an obvious accumulator do not need a written proof; a one-line comment is enough.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insertion sort on [4, 1, 3, 9, 2]. The bracketed prefix a[:i] is always sorted:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Before i = 1: the prefix [4] is sorted (one item). Initialised\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    \u003cmark>[4]\u003c/mark>  1   3   9   2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, x = 1: shift 4 right, drop 1 at index 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    \u003cmark>[1   4]\u003c/mark>  3   9   2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2, x = 3: shift 4 right, 1 is not larger, so drop 3 at index 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    \u003cmark>[1   3   4]\u003c/mark>  9   2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3, x = 9: nothing in the prefix is larger; 9 stays at index 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    \u003cmark>[1   3   4   9]\u003c/mark>  2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, x = 2: shift 9, 4 and 3 right, drop 2 at index 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a    \u003cmark>[1   2   3   4   9]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Exit with i = 5 = len(a): invariant + exit condition = all of a is sorted\u003c/p>\u003cpre>variant n - i went 4, 3, 2, 1, 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>CHECK = True     # turn the invariant checks off once the tests pass\n\n\ndef insertion_sort(a):\n    \"\"\"Sort a in place and return it.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(a)):\n        if CHECK:      # invariant: a[:i] is sorted\n            assert all(a[k] &lt;= a[k + 1] for k in range(i - 1)), (i, a)\n        x, j = a[i], i\n        while j &gt; 0 and a[j - 1] &gt; x:      # variant: j, which falls by 1 and stops at 0\n            a[j] = a[j - 1]\n            j -= 1\n        a[j] = x\n    return a\n\n\ndef floor_sqrt(n):\n    \"\"\"Largest r with r * r &lt;= n, for n &gt;= 0.\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, n + 1            # invariant: the answer is in [lo, hi), i.e. lo*lo &lt;= n &lt; hi*hi\n    while hi - lo &gt; 1:           # variant: hi - lo, which shrinks every iteration\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2     # lo &lt; mid &lt; hi, because hi - lo &gt;= 2\n        if mid * mid &lt;= n:\n            lo = mid\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n\n\ndef pages(n, k):\n    \"\"\"Pages needed for n items, k per page (a fencepost-safe ceiling division).\"\"\"\n    return (n + k - 1) // k\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>An invariant that is true but useless.\u003c/b> \"i &gt;= 0\" is maintained but, combined with the exit condition, proves nothing. The use step is the test of a good invariant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixing conventions.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>hi = len(a) - 1\u003c/code> (closed) with \u003ccode>while lo &lt; hi\u003c/code> (half-open) skips the last element; pick one convention and derive every line from it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No progress on some path.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>lo = mid\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>mid = (lo + hi) // 2\u003c/code> makes no progress when hi = lo + 1. Check that every branch shrinks the variant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariants that hold only \"most of the time\".\u003c/b> It must hold at the top of \u003ci>every\u003c/i> iteration, including the first and after the last.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fenceposts.\u003c/b> n items, n - 1 gaps; the last index is n - 1; \u003ccode>range(n)\u003c/code> stops before n.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Predict the state of insertion sort on [5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3] just before i = 4, and how many shifts the i = 4 step makes.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Before i = 4 the prefix a[:4] is the first four items sorted: [2, 4, 5, 6, 1, 3]. At i = 4, x = 1 is smaller than all four, so 6, 5, 4 and 2 each shift right: 4 shifts, giving [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 3].\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>floor_sqrt\u003c/code> loop on \u003ccode>hi - lo &gt; 1\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>lo &lt; hi\u003c/code>, and what goes wrong with \u003ccode>lo &lt; hi\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its invariant keeps lo itself a valid answer (lo*lo &lt;= n), so the search is done when [lo, hi) holds exactly one candidate. With \u003ccode>lo &lt; hi\u003c/code>, a range of size one gives mid = lo, the branch sets \u003ccode>lo = mid\u003c/code> (no change), and the loop never ends: the variant stops shrinking.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fill in the invariant for a loop that computes the right-to-left running maximum: \u003ccode>best = -1\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1): out[i] = best; best = max(best, a[i])\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Before processing index i, best = max(a[i + 1:]), or -1 when that slice is empty. Initialised (i = n - 1, empty slice, -1); maintained (after writing out[i], best takes a[i] into account, which is max(a[i:]) for the next index); used at each step, since out[i] = max(a[i + 1:]).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1299 Replace Elements with Greatest Element on Right Side\u003c/b>: write the invariant first (the last Check yourself question), then the loop. Expected: [7, 3, 5, 1] gives [5, 5, 1, -1]; [9] gives [-1]; O(n) time with one right-to-left pass, against O(n^2) for the brute force that takes \u003ccode>max(arr[i + 1:])\u003c/code> at every i.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>374 Guess Number Higher or Lower\u003c/b>: keep the candidates in a half-open range [lo, hi) starting at [1, n + 1), and state the invariant and variant before coding. Expected: with n = 10 and pick = 7 your guesses are 6, 9, 8, 7 (four guesses); the worst case is n.bit_length() guesses, 31 for n = 2^31 - 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to prove a loop correct and terminating with an invariant and a variant, and to write ranges half-open.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/how-do-you-rearrange-an-array-in-place-without-l":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An in-place algorithm keeps everything in one array, so the array itself must tell you what is done and what is not. Split it into \u003cb>regions\u003c/b> with indices as boundaries, and write one invariant per region: \"a[:w] holds the kept items, in order\", \"a[:lt] &lt; pivot, a[lt:i] == pivot, a[i:gt] unknown, a[gt:] &gt; pivot\". Every step moves one item out of the unknown region while keeping all the region invariants true.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting a pile of laundry into three baskets is easy. Sorting it on one shelf, without baskets, needs chalk marks: \"left of this mark is done whites, right of that mark is done darks, the middle is unsorted\". The algorithm is just the rules for moving the marks. Lose a mark and you lose track.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Read and write pointers\u003c/h5>\u003cp>To keep only some items, in their original order: the \u003cb>read pointer\u003c/b> r visits every item; the \u003cb>write pointer\u003c/b> w marks where the next kept item goes.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>    a:  [ kept items | already read, discarded | not read yet ]\n          0         w                          r              n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Invariant: \u003ci>a[:w] holds exactly the kept items of a[:r], in their original order.\u003c/i> Initially w = r = 0, both regions empty. Each step reads a[r]: if it is kept, copy it to a[w] and advance w. Since w &lt;= r always, the write never overwrites an unread item. At exit r = n, so a[:w] is the answer and w is its length. This one invariant covers removing a value, removing duplicates from a sorted list (keep a[r] when it differs from the last kept item), moving zeroes to the end and compacting with any predicate.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Three regions: the Dutch national flag\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Rearrange around a pivot into less, equal and greater, in one pass. Four regions, three moving boundaries:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>    a:  [  &lt; pivot  |  == pivot  |  unknown  |  &gt; pivot  ]\n          0         lt           i           gt          n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each step examines a[i], the first unknown item:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>less:\u003c/b> swap it with a[lt] (the first equal item, or a[i] itself when the equal region is empty), then advance lt and i;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>equal:\u003c/b> advance i;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>greater:\u003c/b> decrement gt and swap a[i] with a[gt]. The item that arrives at i came from the unknown region, so \u003cb>do not advance i\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The loop runs while the unknown region [i, gt) is non-empty.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read/write compaction reads every item once and writes at most n times: O(n) time, O(1) extra space. The flag partition shrinks the unknown region gt - i by exactly one per step (either i rises or gt falls), so it takes exactly n steps and at most n swaps: O(n) time, O(1) space. Compare the obvious alternatives: building new lists costs O(n) extra space; calling \u003ccode>list.remove\u003c/code> in a loop costs O(n) per call, O(n^2) in total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The problem says \"in place\" or \"O(1) extra space\", or the data is too large to copy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Filtering or compacting while preserving order: read/write pointers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Partitioning by a key with few values (three-way partition; quicksort with many duplicates; sorting 0, 1, 2 values in one pass).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When not: if order must be preserved \u003ci>and\u003c/i> you are partitioning into several groups, the swap-based partition is not stable (it reorders items within a region); use a stable pass with extra space, or \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> with a key. If extra space is allowed, a list comprehension is clearer: \u003ccode>[x for x in a if keep(x)]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The flag partition on [2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0] with pivot 1. Regions: less is a[:lt], equal a[lt:i], unknown a[i:gt], greater a[gt:]:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: everything is unknown (?). Regions: &lt; is a[:lt], = is a[lt:i], ? is a[i:gt], &gt; is a[gt:]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  0  2  1  1  0\nregion    ?  ?  ?  ?  ?  ?\nlt = 0, i = 0, gt = 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[0] = 2 &gt; pivot: gt = 5, swap a[0] and a[5]; i stays, because the new a[0] is unknown\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  0  2  1  1  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nregion    ?  ?  ?  ?  ?  &gt;\nlt = 0, i = 0, gt = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[0] = 0 &lt; pivot: swap with a[lt] (here itself), then lt = 1, i = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  0  2  1  1  2\nregion    &lt;  ?  ?  ?  ?  &gt;\nlt = 1, i = 1, gt = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[1] = 0 &lt; pivot: swap with a[lt] (here itself), then lt = 2, i = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         0  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  2  1  1  2\nregion    &lt;  &lt;  ?  ?  ?  &gt;\nlt = 2, i = 2, gt = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[2] = 2 &gt; pivot: gt = 4, swap a[2] and a[4]; i stays, because the new a[2] is unknown\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         0  0  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  1  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  2\nregion    &lt;  &lt;  ?  ?  &gt;  &gt;\nlt = 2, i = 2, gt = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[2] = 1 == pivot: i = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         0  0  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  1  2  2\nregion    &lt;  &lt;  =  ?  &gt;  &gt;\nlt = 2, i = 3, gt = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a[3] = 1 == pivot: i = 4. No ? left: return (lt, gt) = (2, 4)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         0  0  1  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  2  2\nregion    &lt;  &lt;  =  =  &gt;  &gt;\nlt = 2, i = 4, gt = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def keep_if(a, keep):\n    \"\"\"Move the items with keep(item) True to the front of a, in order; return their count.\n    Invariant: a[:w] holds exactly the kept items of a[:r], in their original order.\"\"\"\n    w = 0\n    for r in range(len(a)):\n        if keep(a[r]):\n            a[w] = a[r]\n            w += 1\n    return w\n\n\ndef three_way_partition(a, pivot):\n    \"\"\"Rearrange a in place into  &lt; pivot | == pivot | &gt; pivot  and return (lt, gt).\n    Invariant: a[:lt] &lt; pivot, a[lt:i] == pivot, a[i:gt] unknown, a[gt:] &gt; pivot.\"\"\"\n    lt, i, gt = 0, 0, len(a)\n    while i &lt; gt:                         # variant: gt - i, the size of the unknown region\n        if a[i] &lt; pivot:\n            a[lt], a[i] = a[i], a[lt]\n            lt += 1\n            i += 1\n        elif a[i] &gt; pivot:\n            gt -= 1\n            a[i], a[gt] = a[gt], a[i]     # the item swapped in is unknown: do not advance i\n        else:\n            i += 1\n    return lt, gt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Advancing i after swapping with gt.\u003c/b> The item that arrives at i has not been examined; skipping it leaves a large value in the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Writing before reading.\u003c/b> If w could pass r, a write would destroy an unread item; the invariant w &lt;= r is what makes compaction safe.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off-by-one boundaries.\u003c/b> Is gt the first greater item or the last unknown one? Write the region invariant with half-open slices and the code follows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expecting stability.\u003c/b> Swaps reorder equal-region items relative to each other; read/write compaction is stable, the flag partition is not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Returning the wrong thing.\u003c/b> Many in-place problems return a length and judge only \u003ccode>a[:k]\u003c/code>; anything beyond k is free.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fill the missing condition: remove duplicates from a \u003ci>sorted\u003c/i> list in place with \u003ccode>w = 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>for r in range(1, len(a)): if ____: a[w] = a[r]; w += 1\u003c/code> (for non-empty a). What invariant justifies it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>a[r] != a[w - 1]\u003c/code>. Invariant: a[:w] holds the distinct values of a[:r], in order, so a[w - 1] is the last kept value; in a sorted list, a[r] is new exactly when it differs from that value. (Comparing with a[w] instead is a classic bug: a[w] is not a kept item.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace \u003ccode>three_way_partition([1, 2, 0], 1)\u003c/code>: the final list and (lt, gt).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>i = 0: a[0] = 1 is equal, i = 1. a[1] = 2 is greater: gt = 2, swap a[1] and a[2], giving [1, 0, 2]. a[1] = 0 is less: swap with a[lt = 0], giving [0, 1, 2], then lt = 1, i = 2 = gt, stop. Result [0, 1, 2] with (lt, gt) = (1, 2).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the flag partition take exactly n iterations, never more?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each iteration either increases i or decreases gt by one, so the unknown region gt - i shrinks by exactly one per iteration; it starts at n and the loop stops at 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>27 Remove Element\u003c/b>: use read and write pointers with the invariant \"a[:w] holds the items of a[:r] that differ from val\". Expected: [4, 1, 4, 2, 4] with val = 4 gives k = 2 and [1, 2] in the first two slots (your order may differ if you swap from the end); one pass, O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>26 Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array\u003c/b>: the first Check yourself question is the whole idea; write it with its invariant as a comment. Expected: [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 4, 5, 5] gives k = 4 with [0, 1, 4, 5] in the first four slots; O(n) time, O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to keep in-place rearrangements correct by naming each region of the array and the invariant that defines it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/how-do-you-prove-that-a-greedy-choice-is-safe":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A greedy rule (\"always do the shortest job first\") is safe when you can show that \u003cb>any\u003c/b> optimal solution can be changed into the greedy one without getting worse. The \u003cb>exchange argument\u003c/b> does this by swapping two adjacent items that are out of greedy order and showing the cost does not rise; repeat until the order is greedy. \u003cb>Proof by contradiction\u003c/b> shows a property must hold because its failure would allow a strictly better solution. Before proving, let a brute force try to break the rule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At a supermarket with one till, letting the customer with one item go before the customer with a full trolley costs the trolley owner a minute and saves the one-item customer twenty. Total waiting drops. Any queue in which a long job stands just before a short one can be improved by swapping those two, so the best queue has no such pair: it is sorted shortest first.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The exchange argument, step by step\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Jobs have a processing time t and a weight w (importance); a job's completion time C is when it finishes. Minimise the total weighted completion time, the sum of w x C.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Take any order and two \u003cb>adjacent\u003c/b> jobs p then q, starting at time s. Only their own completion times change if you swap them; everything before and after is untouched.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>p first: w_p(s + t_p) + w_q(s + t_p + t_q)\u003c/li>\u003cli>q first: w_q(s + t_q) + w_p(s + t_q + t_p)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The difference (p first minus q first) is \u003cb>w_q t_p - w_p t_q\u003c/b>. So p should go first exactly when t_p w_q &lt;= t_q w_p, that is, when t_p / w_p &lt;= t_q / w_q. The rule: \u003cb>sort by t / w ascending\u003c/b> (Smith's rule, 1956). With equal weights it is \"shortest job first\".\u003c/p>\u003cp>The proof that sorting is optimal: take any optimal order. If it is not sorted by t / w, some adjacent pair is out of order; swapping it does not increase the cost (by the formula). Each swap removes one inversion, so after finitely many swaps the order is sorted, and its cost is no higher than the optimum. So the sorted order is optimal.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Proof by contradiction: why the best block never starts with a negative prefix\u003c/h5>\u003cp>In the maximum-subarray lab, the linear algorithm abandons the running sum when it goes negative. Why is that safe? Suppose an optimal block a[i:j] began with a prefix a[i:k] (k &lt; j) whose sum is negative. Then a[k:j] = (whole) - (prefix) has a strictly larger sum, contradicting optimality. So an optimal block never starts with a negative-sum prefix, and discarding such prefixes loses nothing.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Test before you prove\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A brute force over all orders (n &lt;= 8 is 40,320 orders) checks a candidate rule in seconds on random instances. It quickly rejects tempting wrong rules such as \"heaviest first\" or \"shortest first\" when weights differ, and it gives you confidence before you spend time on a proof.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The greedy algorithm is one sort, O(n log n), plus an O(n) pass to compute the cost. The brute force over all n! orders with an O(n) cost each is O(n x n!), fine as an oracle up to n = 8 or 9. The proof itself is a single algebraic identity plus \"repeat the swap\", the usual size of an exchange argument.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A problem orders or selects items to optimise a sum (scheduling, file placement, interval selection) and a local comparison of two neighbours decides which goes first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>You have a greedy rule that \"feels right\": derive the swap formula, and if it shows that some pair should go the other way, the rule is wrong.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use contradiction for structural facts (an optimal block never starts with a negative prefix; a shortest path never repeats a vertex).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When not: if swapping two items also changes what is feasible later (budgets, capacities, deadlines with penalties), the local swap does not tell the whole story and greedy often fails; that is where dynamic programming takes over. The greedy module returns to exchange arguments and the \"stays ahead\" proof.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Jobs as (t, w): (3, 1), (1, 2), (2, 2), (4, 3), with ratios t / w = 3, 0.5, 1, 1.33. Fix one adjacent inversion at a time and watch the total fall:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start in the given order: total weighted completion time 53\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order   (3,1) (1,2) (2,2) (4,3)\nfinish    3     4     6    10       cost 3 + 8 + 12 + 30 = 53\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">(3,1) before (1,2) is inverted (ratio 3 &gt; 0.5): swap. Change = w_q t_p - w_p t_q = 2*3 - 1*1 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order   \u003cmark>(1,2) (3,1)\u003c/mark> (2,2) (4,3)\nfinish    1     4     6    10       cost \u003cmark>48\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">(3,1) before (2,2) is inverted (3 &gt; 1): swap. Saves 2*3 - 1*2 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order   (1,2) \u003cmark>(2,2) (3,1)\u003c/mark> (4,3)\nfinish    1     3     6    10       cost \u003cmark>44\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">(3,1) before (4,3) is inverted (3 &gt; 1.33): swap. Saves 3*3 - 1*4 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order   (1,2) (2,2) \u003cmark>(4,3) (3,1)\u003c/mark>\nfinish    1     3     7    10       cost \u003cmark>39\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No adjacent pair is inverted: sorted by t / w. A brute force over all 24 orders agrees\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>39 is the minimum\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from functools import cmp_to_key\nfrom itertools import permutations\n\n\ndef weighted_completion(order):\n    \"\"\"Sum of w * (finish time) for jobs (t, w) run in this order.\"\"\"\n    clock = total = 0\n    for t, w in order:\n        clock += t\n        total += w * clock\n    return total\n\n\ndef smith_order(jobs):\n    \"\"\"Optimal order for jobs (t, w) with w &gt; 0: ascending t / w, compared exactly by cross-multiplying.\"\"\"\n    return sorted(jobs, key=cmp_to_key(lambda p, q: p[0] * q[1] - q[0] * p[1]))\n\n\ndef best_cost_brute(jobs):\n    \"\"\"The oracle: try every order (n! of them).\"\"\"\n    return min(weighted_completion(order) for order in permutations(jobs))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Swapping non-adjacent items.\u003c/b> Swapping p and q with jobs between them also shifts those jobs; the clean formula holds for adjacent pairs, which is why the argument works with them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Floating-point ratios.\u003c/b> Sorting by \u003ccode>t / w\u003c/code> as floats can misorder near-equal ratios; compare \u003ccode>t_p * w_q\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>t_q * w_p\u003c/code> in integers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Proving the rule on the examples only.\u003c/b> An exchange argument must work for \u003ci>any\u003c/i> pair in \u003ci>any\u003c/i> optimal solution, not for the sample.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assuming greedy because it worked twice.\u003c/b> Let the brute force search for a counterexample first; it costs minutes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Zero weights.\u003c/b> The ratio t / w is undefined; the cross-multiplied comparison still orders zero-weight jobs last.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With all weights equal to 1, what does the swap formula w_q t_p - w_p t_q reduce to, and what rule follows?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>t_p - t_q. Putting p first is no worse exactly when t_p &lt;= t_q, so the rule is shortest job first.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For the four jobs in the trace, what do \"shortest first\" and \"heaviest first\" cost, and why do they lose to 39?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shortest first, (1,2) (2,2) (3,1) (4,3), costs 2 + 6 + 6 + 30 = 44: it runs the heavy, long job (4,3) last. Heaviest first, (4,3) (1,2) (2,2) (3,1), costs 12 + 10 + 14 + 10 = 46: it makes two light-but-short jobs wait behind a long one. Each rule ignores one of the two quantities in the ratio t / w.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Prove by contradiction that an optimal maximum-sum block never \u003ci>ends\u003c/i> with a suffix whose sum is negative.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suppose an optimal block a[i:j] ends with a suffix a[k:j] (i &lt; k) whose sum is negative. Then a[i:k] has sum (whole) - (suffix), which is strictly larger, contradicting optimality. (Here i &lt; k keeps a[i:k] non-empty.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>smith_order\u003c/code> against \u003ccode>best_cost_brute\u003c/code> on 500 random instances of 1 to 7 jobs with t in 1..9 and w in 1..9, and count mismatches in cost. Expected: 0 mismatches, and for [(3, 1), (1, 2), (2, 2), (4, 3)] both give 39.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace the comparator with \"heaviest first\" (sort by -w) and rerun. Expected: the brute force finds counterexamples on most random instances; the trace's four jobs give 46 against 39.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to prove a greedy ordering with an adjacent exchange argument and a structural shortcut by contradiction.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/how-do-you-find-the-input-that-breaks-your-code-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Bugs live at the edges: empty input, one item, all equal, already sorted, reversed, the largest values, the last element. Attack your code with an \u003cb>edge-case catalogue\u003c/b> before the judge does. When something fails, debug like a scientist: \u003cb>reproduce\u003c/b> with the smallest failing input, \u003cb>print the invariant\u003c/b> each iteration to find where state goes wrong, form \u003cb>one hypothesis\u003c/b>, test it, fix, then rerun every test. Assertions turn invariants into alarms that fire at the first wrong step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A bridge inspector does not drive one family car across and declare it safe. They check the heaviest truck, the strongest wind and the coldest night. Normal inputs rarely break code; extreme and degenerate ones do. Debugging is the same mindset after a failure: you do not stare at the whole program, you narrow the gap between \"where state is still right\" and \"where it is wrong\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The edge-case catalogue\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it breaks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Empty\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[0]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max(a)\u003c/code>, answers that start at 1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One item\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[5]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>loops starting at index 1, pair logic\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[5, 3]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>swaps, neighbour logic\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>All equal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[7, 7, 7]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>strict versus non-strict comparisons, distinctness\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted, reversed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[3, 2, 1]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>best and worst cases, pivots\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extremes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0, negatives, 10^9, n at its maximum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sentinels, overflow in C++/Java, time limits\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The last element\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the answer sits at the end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>updates that run only when something changes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>spaces only, trailing spaces, one character\u003c/td>\u003ctd>split and strip logic, empty words\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Generate the catalogue in code (the template) and run every case through both your fast solution and a brute force.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Debugging, step by step\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reproduce\u003c/b> the failure with a fixed input, and \u003cb>shrink\u003c/b> it: delete items and simplify values while it still fails. A failure on \u003ccode>[7, 7, 7]\u003c/code> is far easier to read than one on 10,000 random numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Print the invariant\u003c/b>, not random variables. If the loop claims \"best is the longest run that has ended before i, cur is the run ending at i\", print i, best and cur each iteration and find the first line where the claim is false.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One hypothesis at a time.\u003c/b> Write it down (\"the last run is never compared\"), predict what a change will do, make one change, and rerun.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix, then rerun everything\u003c/b>: the catalogue, the stress test and the examples. Fixes often break a different edge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>For regressions\u003c/b>, binary-search history: \u003ccode>git bisect run ./test.sh\u003c/code> finds the first commit whose test fails.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>Checklists by verdict\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrong answer:\u003c/b> edge cases above; a misread statement (strict or not, indices or values, 0- or 1-based); the last element or last group never processed; state left over between test cases (globals, class attributes, mutable default arguments); integer division \u003ccode>//\u003c/code> with negatives; comparing floats with ==.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Runtime error:\u003c/b> index out of range at a boundary; \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> where a value was expected; a missing dict key; \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> (CPython's default limit is 1,000 frames); modifying a list or dict while iterating over it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Time limit:\u003c/b> the constraint budget was not met; hidden O(n) inside a loop (\u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list.insert(0, x)\u003c/code>, slicing, string \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code>); recursion without memoisation; printing debug output in a hot loop; a loop that never ends (check the variant).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Assertions as executable invariants\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>assert cur &gt;= 1, (i, cur)\u003c/code> stops the program at the first broken step, with the state in the message. Python removes all asserts when run with \u003ccode>-O\u003c/code>, so never use them for input validation, only for \"this cannot happen\" checks during development. SQLite takes the same line: thousands of asserts in debug builds, compiled out of production builds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The catalogue is about ten tiny inputs: microseconds to run and minutes to write once, then reusable for every list problem. Shrinking by hand costs a few minutes; the stress card automates it. Printing the invariant costs one line. The expensive path is the alternative: guessing, changing three things at once and resubmitting.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Before every submission: run the catalogue against your brute force.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After any failure: the five debugging steps, in order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assertions whenever a loop has an invariant you could state; remove or disable them once the tests pass if they cost more than O(1).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When not: hand-picked edges complement random testing, they do not replace it. Interactions between features (duplicates \u003ci>and\u003c/i> negatives at the end) are what random testing finds better.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003ccode>longest_run\u003c/code> that returns \u003ccode>best\u003c/code> after the loop (the template's buggy version), debugged on [3, 7, 7, 7] by printing its invariant:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reproduce: the catalogue's \"all equal\" case already fails\u003c/p>\u003cpre>longest_run([7, 7, 7]) = 1, brute force says 3\n\u003cmark>also fails: [] gives 1, want 0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Use a slightly richer failing input and print the invariant\u003c/p>\u003cpre>claim: best = longest run that ended before i,\n       cur  = length of the run ending at i\nxs = [3, 7, 7, 7]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: 7 != 3, a run ended: best = max(1, 1) = 1, cur = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i  xs[i]  best  cur\n1    7     1     1      claim holds\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2 and i = 3: equal neighbours, cur grows\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i  xs[i]  best  cur\n2    7     1     2\n3    7     1     \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    claim holds\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Loop ends; the function returns best = 1. The run 7 7 7 never \"ended\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>hypothesis: the final run is never compared with best\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Fix: return max(best, cur), and 0 for empty input. Rerun everything\u003c/p>\u003cpre>catalogue: 0 failures\n\u003cmark>stress test against the brute force: passes\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def edge_lists(lo=-3, hi=3):\n    \"\"\"The edge-case catalogue for a single list argument.\"\"\"\n    yield []                                   # empty\n    yield [lo]                                 # one item\n    yield [lo, hi]                             # two items\n    yield [hi, lo]\n    yield [hi] * 4                             # all equal\n    yield list(range(lo, hi + 1))              # sorted\n    yield list(range(hi, lo - 1, -1))          # reversed\n    yield [0, hi, 0, hi, 0]                    # alternating repeats\n    yield [lo] * 2 + [hi] * 3                  # the longest run is last\n\n\ndef check_edges(fast, brute, cases):\n    \"\"\"Run every case through both versions; return (input, want, got) for each mismatch.\"\"\"\n    failures = []\n    for xs in cases:\n        want = brute(list(xs))\n        try:\n            got = fast(list(xs))\n        except Exception as e:                 # a crash is a result too\n            got = repr(e)\n        if got != want:\n            failures.append((xs, want, got))\n    return failures\n\n\ndef longest_run_brute(xs):\n    n = len(xs)\n    return max((j - i for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n + 1) if len(set(xs[i:j])) == 1), default=0)\n\n\ndef longest_run_buggy(xs):\n    best = cur = 1\n    for i in range(1, len(xs)):\n        if xs[i] == xs[i - 1]:\n            cur += 1\n        else:\n            best = max(best, cur)\n            cur = 1\n    return best\n\n\nfor case in check_edges(longest_run_buggy, longest_run_brute, edge_lists()):\n    print(case)\n# ([], 0, 1)\n# ([3, 3, 3, 3], 4, 1)\n# ([-3, -3, 3, 3, 3], 3, 2)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Changing several things at once.\u003c/b> If the test then passes you do not know which change fixed it, or what else you broke.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Debugging the big input.\u003c/b> Shrink first; a five-item failure is usually readable at a glance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Printing everything.\u003c/b> Print the invariant's variables at the top of each iteration; the first line where the claim fails points at the bug.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Asserts for input validation.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>python -O\u003c/code> deletes them; raise \u003ccode>ValueError\u003c/code> for bad input instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stopping at the first fix.\u003c/b> Rerun the catalogue and the stress test; a fix for the empty case often breaks the one-item case.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which catalogue cases expose \u003ccode>def last_word_length(s): return len(s.split(\" \")[-1])\u003c/code>, and what does it return on them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Trailing spaces: \"fly me \" splits on single spaces into ['fly', 'me', ''], so it returns 0. A string of spaces returns 0 for the same reason. \u003ccode>s.split()\u003c/code> with no argument splits on runs of whitespace and drops empty pieces, which fixes it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A solution passes all samples but gets a time limit on n = 10^5. The loop body contains \u003ccode>if x in seen:\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>seen = []\u003c/code>. Explain and fix.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> scans the list: O(n) per check, O(n^2) overall, about 10^10 steps. Make \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> a set, so each check is O(1) on average and the loop is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A plus-one function on a list of digits passes [1, 2, 3] and [4, 9]. Name the catalogue case most likely to break it and the expected output.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>All digits 9, the extreme carry: [9] should give [1, 0] and [9, 9] should give [1, 0, 0]. Code that only propagates the carry inside the list forgets the new leading digit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>66 Plus One\u003c/b>: write the catalogue for digit lists first ([0], [9], [9, 9], [1, 9], [8, 9, 9]), with Python's integers as the oracle (\u003ccode>int(\"\".join(map(str, d))) + 1\u003c/code>). Expected: [9, 9] gives [1, 0, 0], [1, 2, 9] gives [1, 3, 0], [0] gives [1]; O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>58 Length of Last Word\u003c/b>: run your solution on the string catalogue (one letter, trailing spaces, several spaces between words, leading spaces) against the oracle \u003ccode>len(s.split()[-1])\u003c/code>. Expected: \"  fly to   the moon  \" gives 4, \"a\" gives 1, \"a \" gives 1; an O(1)-space solution scans from the end, skipping spaces, then counting letters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to attack code with an edge-case catalogue and debug a failure by reproducing, shrinking and checking the invariant one hypothesis at a time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/how-do-you-stress-test-a-solution-against-a-brut":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the slow, obviously correct solution and let the computer search for disagreements: \u003cb>generate\u003c/b> a small random input, run the \u003cb>brute force\u003c/b> and the \u003cb>fast\u003c/b> version, \u003cb>compare\u003c/b>, and stop at the first mismatch. Then \u003cb>shrink\u003c/b> it: keep deleting items and moving numbers towards zero while the two versions still disagree. Thousands of small random cases find most bugs in seconds, and the shrunk case usually shows the bug at a glance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A new recipe and a trusted one should give the same cake. You do not need to know which step of the new recipe is wrong to discover \u003ci>that\u003c/i> something is wrong: bake both a thousand times with random ingredients and compare. When they differ, bake again with fewer and fewer ingredients until removing anything makes the difference disappear. What is left points at the bug.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The four parts\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generator:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>gen(rng)\u003c/code> returns a tuple of arguments. Keep sizes small (0 to 8 items) and value ranges narrow (-5 to 5), so duplicates, ties, zeros and negatives turn up constantly. Pass a seeded \u003ccode>random.Random\u003c/code>, so every failure can be reproduced.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Oracle:\u003c/b> the brute force from the solving loop, independent of the fast code.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compare:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> by default; a custom \u003ccode>same\u003c/code> for answers that may differ but both be valid (or compare canonical forms, such as sorted lists). A crash in the fast version counts as a mismatch; a crash in the oracle is reported as a broken oracle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shrink:\u003c/b> from the failing input, try simpler candidates (delete everything, then halves, then single items; replace numbers by 0, by half, or by one step towards 0) and move to the first one that still fails; repeat until no candidate fails. The result is \u003cb>locally minimal\u003c/b>: no single simplification still fails.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>In-place functions are wrapped so the harness sees everything the contract promises: for \"remove duplicates and return k\", the wrapper returns \u003ccode>nums[:k]\u003c/code> (and k), not just k. The harness passes deep copies, so an in-place function cannot corrupt the input it is compared on. If a precondition must hold (the list is sorted), pass \u003ccode>valid=\u003c/code> so shrinking never produces an invalid input.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>stress.py\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Save this once and reuse it in every lab of this guide. It is also the first piece of dsakit, the toolkit project that ends this stage.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"stress.py: compare a fast solution with a brute-force oracle on random inputs, then shrink the first failure.\n\n    from stress import stress\n    stress(gen, brute, fast)      # gen(rng) returns a tuple of arguments\n\nA crash in `fast` counts as a failure; a crash in `brute` is reported as a broken oracle.\n\"\"\"\nimport copy\nimport random\n\n\ndef run(fn, args):\n    \"\"\"Call fn on a fresh deep copy of args, so in-place functions cannot corrupt the test case.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        return \"ok\", fn(*copy.deepcopy(args))\n    except Exception as e:\n        return \"crash\", repr(e)\n\n\ndef simpler(x):\n    \"\"\"Yield values a little simpler than x: shorter lists and strings, numbers nearer 0.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(x, bool):\n        if x:\n            yield False\n    elif isinstance(x, int):\n        for y in dict.fromkeys((0, x // 2 if x &gt; 0 else -(-x // 2), x - 1 if x &gt; 0 else x + 1)):\n            if abs(y) &lt; abs(x):\n                yield y\n    elif isinstance(x, (list, str)):\n        size = len(x)\n        while size &gt;= 1:                          # delete chunks: everything, halves, ..., single items\n            for start in range(0, len(x) - size + 1, size):\n                yield x[:start] + x[start + size:]\n            size //= 2\n        if isinstance(x, list):\n            for i, item in enumerate(x):\n                for y in simpler(item):\n                    yield x[:i] + [y] + x[i + 1:]\n    elif isinstance(x, tuple):                    # an argument tuple: simplify one argument at a time\n        for i, item in enumerate(x):\n            for y in simpler(item):\n                yield x[:i] + (y,) + x[i + 1:]\n\n\ndef shrink(args, still_fails):\n    \"\"\"Greedily move to a simpler input that still fails, until no simpler candidate fails.\"\"\"\n    improved = True\n    while improved:\n        improved = False\n        for candidate in simpler(args):\n            if still_fails(candidate):\n                args, improved = candidate, True\n                break\n    return args\n\n\ndef stress(gen, brute, fast, trials=2000, seed=0, same=None, valid=None):\n    \"\"\"Run fast and brute on trials random inputs; on the first mismatch print a shrunk case and return it.\"\"\"\n    same = same or (lambda want, got: want == got)\n\n    def fails(args):\n        if valid is not None and not valid(*args):\n            return False                           # shrinking must keep the precondition true\n        want, got = run(brute, args), run(fast, args)\n        if want[0] == \"crash\":\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"the oracle crashed on {args!r}: {want[1]}\")\n        return got[0] == \"crash\" or not same(want[1], got[1])\n\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    for trial in range(trials):\n        args = gen(rng)\n        if fails(args):\n            small = shrink(args, fails)\n            print(f\"FAIL on trial {trial} (seed {seed})\")\n            print(f\"  found:  {args!r}\")\n            print(f\"  shrunk: {small!r}\")\n            print(f\"  brute:  {run(brute, small)[1]!r}\")\n            print(f\"  fast:   {run(fast, small)[1]!r}\")\n            return small\n    print(f\"OK: {trials} random tests passed (seed {seed})\")\n    return None\n\n\ndef rand_list(rng, max_len=8, lo=-5, hi=5):\n    \"\"\"A short list of small integers: small ranges force duplicates, ties and equal neighbours.\"\"\"\n    return [rng.randint(lo, hi) for _ in range(rng.randint(0, max_len))]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each trial costs one brute-force call plus one fast call. With inputs of at most 8 items, even an O(n^3) brute force does a few hundred steps, so 2,000 trials take well under a second in CPython. Shrinking is greedy: each accepted step strictly shortens the input or brings a number closer to 0, so it terminates, and each round tries O(size) candidates; on small inputs it finishes in milliseconds. Deep copies add O(input size) per call, negligible at these sizes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Whenever an obviously correct slow solution exists: almost every algorithm in this guide. It is the default check in every later lab.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Before submitting a tricky solution, and immediately when a hidden test fails.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For refactors: keep the old version as the oracle while you optimise.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>When not: it cannot find bugs that need large inputs (overflow at 2^31, recursion depth, time limits); add a few large targeted tests and a timing run. If no brute force exists, test properties instead (next card). A random test that passes is evidence, not proof: pair it with the invariant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The harness on \u003ccode>second_largest\u003c/code> with a planted bug: the \u003ccode>elif\u003c/code> branch lost its \u003ccode>x != first\u003c/code> check (the restate card in the method module shows the correct version).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Trial 0: both versions agree\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = [1, -5, -1, 3, 2, 1]           brute 2     fast 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Trial 1: both agree\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = [2, 0, 4, -2]                  brute 2     fast 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Trial 2: mismatch. Stop and shrink\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = [-3, -1, -3, -4, 4, -1, 3, 4]  brute 3     fast \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Delete everything: [] passes (both None). Delete the first half: still fails, keep it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = \u003cmark>[4, -1, 3, 4]\u003c/mark>                  brute 3     fast 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Delete single items: removing -1 still fails, keep it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = \u003cmark>[4, 3, 4]\u003c/mark>                      brute 3     fast 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Removing 3 still fails, keep it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>xs = \u003cmark>[4, 4]\u003c/mark>                         brute None  fast 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No simpler candidate fails: [4], [], [0, 4], [2, 4], [3, 4] all pass\u003c/p>\u003cpre>shrunk: [4, 4]   \u003cmark>a repeated maximum is counted as second\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The real output of \u003ccode>stress(lambda r: (rand_list(r),), second_largest_brute, buggy)\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>FAIL on trial 2 (seed 0)\n  found:  ([-3, -1, -3, -4, 4, -1, 3, 4],)\n  shrunk: ([4, 4],)\n  brute:  None\n  fast:   4\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Using the harness: a generator, an oracle, the fast version and, for in-place code, a wrapper that returns everything the contract promises.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from stress import rand_list, stress\n\n\ndef dedupe_sorted(a):\n    \"\"\"Remove repeats from sorted a in place; return k, with a[:k] holding each value once.\"\"\"\n    if not a:\n        return 0\n    w = 1\n    for r in range(1, len(a)):\n        if a[r] != a[w - 1]:\n            a[w] = a[r]\n            w += 1\n    return w\n\n\ndef dedupe_fast(a):                  # wrapper: compare the whole promised result\n    k = dedupe_sorted(a)\n    return k, a[:k]\n\n\ndef dedupe_brute(a):\n    distinct = sorted(set(a))\n    return len(distinct), distinct\n\n\nstress(lambda rng: (sorted(rand_list(rng)),), dedupe_brute, dedupe_fast,\n       valid=lambda a: a == sorted(a))\n# OK: 2000 random tests passed (seed 0)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Large random inputs.\u003c/b> Big inputs make the brute force slow and the failures unreadable; small sizes with narrow value ranges find more bugs per second.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unseeded randomness.\u003c/b> Without a seed you cannot rerun the failure; the harness prints the seed and the trial.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>An oracle that shares code\u003c/b> (or the same misreading of the statement) with the fast version: both agree on the wrong answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing too little.\u003c/b> For in-place functions, compare the array contents the contract promises, not just the returned length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Treating \"OK\" as proof.\u003c/b> Random tests miss what they never generate: values near 2^31, n = 10^5, deep recursion. Add those as targeted tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A hanging fast version.\u003c/b> The harness waits forever on an infinite loop; print the trial's input before running it, or add a step counter, to find which input hangs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The shrinker stops at \u003ccode>([0, -1], 5)\u003c/code> for a rotate function. Why can it not reach \u003ccode>([0, -1], 3)\u003c/code>, which also fails, and is that a problem?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>From 5 it only tries 0, 2 and 4 (zero, half, one step towards zero); with two items those rotations equal 0, 0 and 0 steps, so all pass. 3 is not a single simplification away, so 5 is locally minimal. That is fine: the case is already tiny, and you can finish by hand (rotating two items by 5 should equal rotating by 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>run\u003c/code> deep-copy the arguments before every call?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In-place functions modify their input. Without copies, the fast version would receive a list the brute force had already changed (or the reverse), and the comparison, and any shrinking, would be on corrupted inputs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your generator draws lists of length 0 to 1,000 with values up to 10^9, and the stress test finds nothing. What would you change?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shrink the ranges: lengths 0 to 8 and values -5 to 5. Small values force duplicates, zeros and ties, where most bugs live; small lengths make each trial cheap (more trials per second) and keep the brute force fast.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>26 Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array\u003c/b>: plug your solution into the template above (wrap it so it returns \u003ccode>(k, nums[:k])\u003c/code>), then break it on purpose by comparing \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>a[w]\u003c/code> and rerun. Expected: \u003ccode>OK: 2000 random tests passed (seed 0)\u003c/code> for the correct version; the broken one shrinks to \u003ccode>([0, 1],)\u003c/code>, where it returns k = 1 instead of 2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1299 Replace Elements with Greatest Element on Right Side\u003c/b>: stress your O(n) right-to-left pass against \u003ccode>[max(arr[i + 1:], default=-1) for i in range(len(arr))]\u003c/code> with values from 1 to 9 (non-empty lists, so pass \u003ccode>valid=lambda arr: len(arr) &gt; 0\u003c/code>). Expected: \u003ccode>OK: 2000 random tests passed\u003c/code>; [7, 3, 5, 1] gives [5, 5, 1, -1].\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to stress-test any solution against a brute force with a reusable harness that stops at the first failure and shrinks it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:correct/what-does-property-based-testing-add-and-how-do-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>property\u003c/b> is a statement that must hold for every valid input: \"the output is ordered and is a permutation of the input\", \"decode(encode(s)) == s\", \"my answer equals the brute force's\". \u003cb>Hypothesis\u003c/b> generates hundreds of inputs per property, including nasty ones you would not think of, and when one fails it \u003cb>shrinks\u003c/b> it to a minimal example and remembers it for the next run. Use it when you can state properties, even without a brute force.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An example test asks \"does 2 + 3 give 5?\". A property test asks \"is a + b always equal to b + a?\", and lets a machine pick the a and b. You stop guessing which examples matter and write down what must always be true. The machine is good at picking examples: empty strings, enormous integers, Unicode digits, lists of 50 equal items.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Properties that work for algorithms\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Property\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Agrees with an oracle\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>my_sort(xs) == sorted(xs)\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>fast(a) == brute(a)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Postcondition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>output ordered, and \u003ccode>Counter(out) == Counter(xs)\u003c/code> (a permutation)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Round trip\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>decode(encode(s)) == s\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>deserialise(serialise(tree)) == tree\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Idempotent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>my_sort(my_sort(xs)) == my_sort(xs)\u003c/code>; normalising twice changes nothing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Invariant preserved\u003c/td>\u003ctd>after every operation, the heap property still holds\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Metamorphic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>shuffling the input does not change the answer of a sort or a sum\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Writing a test\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>@given(strategy)\u003c/code> feeds generated values into a test function; pytest runs it. Strategies describe valid inputs:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>st.integers(-5, 5)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>st.lists(st.integers(), max_size=30)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>st.text(alphabet=\"ab \")\u003c/code>;\u003c/li>\u003cli>preconditions by construction: \u003ccode>st.lists(st.integers()).map(sorted)\u003c/code> gives sorted lists;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>assume(cond)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>.filter(cond)\u003c/code> to discard rare invalid inputs (prefer construction: heavy filtering slows generation).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Shrinking and the example database\u003c/h5>\u003cp>When a property fails, Hypothesis shrinks the input towards the simplest version that still fails (shorter lists, smaller numbers, simpler characters) and reports it as the failing test case. It saves the failure in a local \u003ccode>.hypothesis\u003c/code> directory and replays it first on the next run, so a fixed bug stays fixed. Add \u003ccode>@example(...)\u003c/code> to always include specific edge cases, and \u003ccode>@settings(max_examples=1000)\u003c/code> to search harder.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>A real run\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The test file below checks four properties of insertion sort (they pass) and the round trip of a run-length encoder that writes \"aaab\" as \"3a1b\". In one derandomised run, Hypothesis reported \u003cb>two distinct failures\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>s='0'\u003c/code>, where the encoding \"10\" is read back as a count of ten followed by nothing, and \u003ccode>s='²:'\u003c/code>, where \u003ccode>str.isdigit()\u003c/code> accepts the superscript ² but \u003ccode>int()\u003c/code> rejects it. The second is a Unicode corner that hand-written examples almost never cover.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>By default each \u003ccode>@given\u003c/code> test runs 100 examples, plus the shrinking steps after a failure; for small algorithmic inputs that is well under a second per property. \u003ccode>max_examples\u003c/code> trades time for thoroughness. Strategies with heavy filtering or large sizes slow down generation; Hypothesis's health checks fail the test when it has to reject too many inputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>When you can state properties but have no brute force (encoders, parsers, data structures).\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the input space is structured (Unicode text, nested lists, floats with NaN and infinities): Hypothesis's strategies explore it better than \u003ccode>random.randint\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For data structures, its stateful testing drives random sequences of operations against a simple model; the design module uses it for an LRU cache.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>stress.py\u003c/th>\u003cth>Hypothesis\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Needs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a brute-force oracle\u003c/td>\u003ctd>any property (an oracle is one kind)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>your generator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>rich strategies, edge-biased\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shrinking\u003c/td>\u003ctd>simple greedy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sophisticated, per strategy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Remembers failures\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no (rerun with the seed)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes, in \u003ccode>.hypothesis\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Dependencies\u003c/td>\u003ctd>none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pip install hypothesis pytest\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both are worth having: the harness is one short file you understand fully and can run anywhere; Hypothesis is stronger at finding and minimising strange inputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Failing inputs Hypothesis tried for the round-trip property during one shrinking run (encoder output and decoder output in brackets; the decoder should return the input):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First failure: a nine-character string of random Unicode containing the digit 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the encoder writes \"19\" for the run \"9\"; the decoder glues those digits\nto the next count, reads \"191\" and prints 191 copies of the next character\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A smaller failure: four zeros\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s = '0000'   encode -&gt; '40'    decode -&gt; ''\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Two characters, a digit then a letter\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s = '0ú'     encode -&gt; '101ú'  decode -&gt; 101 copies of 'ú'\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Two zeros\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s = '00'     encode -&gt; '20'    decode -&gt; ''\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Nothing simpler fails: reported as the failing test case\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>s = '0'      encode -&gt; '10'    decode -&gt; ''\u003c/mark>\nthe format cannot tell a count's digits from data digits\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Save as \u003ccode>test_props.py\u003c/code> and run \u003ccode>pytest test_props.py\u003c/code> (after \u003ccode>pip install hypothesis pytest\u003c/code>):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\nfrom hypothesis import given, strategies as st\n\n\ndef insertion_sort(a):\n    \"\"\"Sort a in place and return it.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(a)):\n        x, j = a[i], i\n        while j &gt; 0 and a[j - 1] &gt; x:\n            a[j] = a[j - 1]\n            j -= 1\n        a[j] = x\n    return a\n\n\n@given(st.lists(st.integers()))\ndef test_sort_properties(xs):\n    out = insertion_sort(list(xs))\n    assert all(p &lt;= q for p, q in zip(out, out[1:]))      # ordered\n    assert Counter(out) == Counter(xs)                    # a permutation of the input\n    assert insertion_sort(list(out)) == out               # idempotent\n    assert out == sorted(xs)                              # agrees with a trusted oracle\n\n\ndef rle_encode(s):\n    \"\"\"'aaabcc' -&gt; '3a1b2c': each run becomes its length followed by its character.\"\"\"\n    out, i = [], 0\n    while i &lt; len(s):\n        j = i\n        while j &lt; len(s) and s[j] == s[i]:\n            j += 1\n        out.append(f\"{j - i}{s[i]}\")\n        i = j\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\ndef rle_decode(code):\n    out, count = [], \"\"\n    for ch in code:\n        if ch.isdigit():\n            count += ch\n        else:\n            out.append(ch * int(count))\n            count = \"\"\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\n\n@given(st.text())\ndef test_rle_round_trip(s):\n    assert rle_decode(rle_encode(s)) == s     # fails: Hypothesis reports s='0'\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-implementing the code in the test.\u003c/b> A property that recomputes the answer the same way shares the bug; state relations (ordered, permutation, round trip) or use a genuinely different oracle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Too-weak properties.\u003c/b> \"Output is ordered\" alone passes \u003ccode>return []\u003c/code>; combine properties.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Filtering instead of constructing.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>.filter(is_sorted)\u003c/code> on random lists rejects almost everything; \u003ccode>.map(sorted)\u003c/code> builds valid inputs directly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unbounded sizes for slow code.\u003c/b> An O(n^2) function on lists of thousands of items makes tests slow; set \u003ccode>max_size\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deleting \u003ccode>.hypothesis\u003c/code>, or ignoring a flaky report.\u003c/b> The saved examples are regression tests; a failure that \"goes away\" on rerun usually depends on hidden state.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>test_sort_properties\u003c/code> check both \"ordered\" and \"permutation\" when it also compares with \u003ccode>sorted(xs)\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The oracle comparison alone is enough for correctness here, but the separate properties say \u003ci>which\u003c/i> promise broke when it fails, and they still work in projects where no trusted oracle exists. Each property alone is too weak (an empty output is ordered; the input itself is a permutation).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you make the RLE format survive digits in the text, and which property proves the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Make the format unambiguous: write the character first, then its count, then a separator, so \"aaab\" becomes \"a3;b1;\". The decoder takes exactly one character (whatever it is, digits and separators included), then reads the digits up to the next \";\". The same round-trip property, \u003ccode>rle_decode(rle_encode(s)) == s\u003c/code> over \u003ccode>st.text()\u003c/code>, then passes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write a strategy for valid inputs to \"remove duplicates from a sorted list\" with values from -100 to 100.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>st.lists(st.integers(-100, 100), min_size=1).map(sorted)\u003c/code>: generate any list in range, then sort it, so every input meets the precondition without rejections.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>66 Plus One\u003c/b>: write the property \"as an integer, the output is the input plus one, and it has no leading zero\" with the strategy \u003ccode>st.lists(st.integers(0, 9), min_size=1).filter(lambda d: d[0] != 0 or len(d) == 1)\u003c/code>. Expected: your correct solution passes; a version that forgets the new leading digit fails and Hypothesis shrinks it to \u003ccode>d=[9]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>26 Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array\u003c/b>: properties \"a[:k] is strictly increasing\" and \"set(a[:k]) == set(input)\" over \u003ccode>st.lists(st.integers(-100, 100), min_size=1).map(sorted)\u003c/code>. Expected: the correct version passes 100 examples; comparing \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>a[w]\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>a[w - 1]\u003c/code> fails with \u003ccode>a=[0, 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to state properties of an algorithm and let Hypothesis search for, and shrink, the inputs that break them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:correct/break-it-then-prove-it":"\u003cp>Five short functions each pass a quick read and a couple of examples. Your job: let \u003ccode>stress.py\u003c/code> find and shrink a failing input for each, then repair each function by stating the invariant it should keep and deriving the fix from it, not by trial and error.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or later; \u003ccode>stress.py\u003c/code> from the stress-testing card, in the same folder.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two new files: \u003ccode>buggy.py\u003c/code> (the functions) and \u003ccode>break_it.py\u003c/code> (the oracles and the stress calls).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save the five functions as \u003ccode>buggy.py\u003c/code>. Read each one and predict, before running anything, whether you think it is correct.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"Five functions that pass a quick glance and fail somewhere. Find a failing input for each.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef partition(a, pivot):\n    \"\"\"Rearrange a in place so that a[:k] &lt; pivot &lt;= a[k:]; return k.\"\"\"\n    i, j = 0, len(a) - 1\n    while i &lt; j:\n        if a[i] &lt; pivot:\n            i += 1\n        elif a[j] &gt;= pivot:\n            j -= 1\n        else:\n            a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]\n    return i\n\n\ndef merge(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Merge two sorted lists into one sorted list.\"\"\"\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(a) or j &lt; len(b):\n        if j == len(b) or a[i] &lt;= b[j]:\n            out.append(a[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(b[j])\n            j += 1\n    return out\n\n\ndef dedupe(a):\n    \"\"\"Remove repeats from sorted a in place; return k so that a[:k] holds each value once.\"\"\"\n    if not a:\n        return 0\n    w = 1\n    for r in range(1, len(a)):\n        if a[r] != a[w]:\n            a[w] = a[r]\n            w += 1\n    return w\n\n\ndef rotate(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Return a copy of a rotated right by k &gt;= 0 steps: rotate([1, 2, 3], 1) == [3, 1, 2].\"\"\"\n    if not a:\n        return []\n    return a[-k:] + a[:-k]\n\n\ndef lower_bound(a, x):\n    \"\"\"First index i with a[i] &gt;= x in sorted a, or len(a) if there is none.\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\n    while lo &lt; hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if a[mid] &gt;= x:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Save the oracles and stress calls as \u003ccode>break_it.py\u003c/code>. Each oracle is written the obvious way; the wrappers report everything a contract promises.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import bisect\n\nimport buggy\nfrom stress import rand_list, stress\n\n\ndef is_sorted(a):\n    return all(p &lt;= q for p, q in zip(a, a[1:]))\n\n\ndef partition_brute(a, pivot):\n    small = [x for x in a if x &lt; pivot]\n    return len(small), sorted(small), sorted(x for x in a if x &gt;= pivot)\n\n\ndef partition_fast(a, pivot):\n    k = buggy.partition(a, pivot)\n    return k, sorted(a[:k]), sorted(a[k:])\n\n\ndef dedupe_fast(a):\n    k = buggy.dedupe(a)\n    return a[:k]\n\n\ndef rotate_brute(a, k):\n    out = list(a)\n    for _ in range(k):                   # one step at a time: obviously right\n        out = out[-1:] + out[:-1]\n    return out\n\n\nstress(lambda r: (rand_list(r), r.randint(-5, 5)), partition_brute, partition_fast)\nstress(lambda r: (sorted(rand_list(r)), sorted(rand_list(r))), lambda a, b: sorted(a + b), buggy.merge,\n       valid=lambda a, b: is_sorted(a) and is_sorted(b))\nstress(lambda r: (sorted(rand_list(r)),), lambda a: sorted(set(a)), dedupe_fast, valid=is_sorted)\nstress(lambda r: (rand_list(r), r.randint(0, 12)), rotate_brute, buggy.rotate, valid=lambda a, k: k &gt;= 0)\nstress(lambda r: (sorted(rand_list(r)), r.randint(-6, 6)), bisect.bisect_left, buggy.lower_bound,\n       valid=lambda a, x: is_sorted(a))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python break_it.py\u003c/code>. For each FAIL, copy the shrunk input and work out by hand what the function does on it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each function, \u003cb>write the invariant first\u003c/b> as a comment (what is true of i, j, w, lo, hi at the top of every iteration), then change only the lines that break it. The Check yourself answers below give the fixes; try each before you look.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rerun \u003ccode>python break_it.py\u003c/code> until all five print \u003ccode>OK\u003c/code>. Then raise \u003ccode>trials\u003c/code> to 20,000 and try other seeds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write one journal line per bug: the edge it lived on (empty, one item, equal values, k &gt;= n, the last index).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With seed 0, five failures, each shrunk to a tiny case:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>partition\u003c/code>: shrunk to \u003ccode>([0], 1)\u003c/code>. It returns k = 0, but 0 &lt; 1 belongs on the left.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>merge\u003c/code>: shrunk to \u003ccode>([], [0])\u003c/code>, and the fast version crashes with \u003ccode>IndexError\u003c/code>. A crash counts as a failure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>dedupe\u003c/code>: shrunk to \u003ccode>([0, 1],)\u003c/code>. It returns \u003ccode>[0]\u003c/code>, losing the 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>rotate\u003c/code>: shrunk to \u003ccode>([0, -1], 5)\u003c/code>. Rotating two items by 5 should equal rotating by 1, giving \u003ccode>[-1, 0]\u003c/code>; it returns the list unchanged. The shrinker stops at 5 because 0, 2 and 4 (its only simplifications) all pass.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>lower_bound\u003c/code>: shrunk to \u003ccode>([0], 1)\u003c/code>. It returns 0; the answer is 1 (past the end).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>After the fixes, each line reads \u003ccode>OK: 2000 random tests passed (seed 0)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fix \u003ccode>partition\u003c/code> by writing its invariant first.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use half-open regions: \u003ccode>i, j = 0, len(a)\u003c/code> with the invariant a[:i] &lt; pivot, a[j:] &gt;= pivot, a[i:j] unknown. Loop \u003ccode>while i &lt; j\u003c/code>: if \u003ccode>a[i] &lt; pivot\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>i += 1\u003c/code>; otherwise \u003ccode>j -= 1\u003c/code> and swap a[i] with a[j]. Return i. The original closed version stopped with one unexamined item when i met j.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fix \u003ccode>merge\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Invariant: out is the sorted merge of a[:i] and b[:j]. Take from a only when a still has items and either b is exhausted or a[i] &lt;= b[j]: \u003ccode>if i &lt; len(a) and (j == len(b) or a[i] &lt;= b[j]):\u003c/code>. The original read a[i] after a was exhausted.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fix \u003ccode>dedupe\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>rotate\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>dedupe\u003c/code>: the invariant is \"a[:w] holds the distinct values of a[:r]\", so the last kept value is a[w - 1]: compare \u003ccode>a[r] != a[w - 1]\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>rotate\u003c/code>: rotating by k equals rotating by k % n, so set \u003ccode>k %= len(a)\u003c/code> and return \u003ccode>a[len(a) - k:] + a[:len(a) - k]\u003c/code>, which also avoids the \u003ccode>a[-0:]\u003c/code> trap when k becomes 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Fix \u003ccode>lower_bound\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The answer can be len(a), so the candidate range is the half-open [0, len(a) + 1), expressed as \u003ccode>lo, hi = 0, len(a)\u003c/code> with the invariant \"everything left of lo is &lt; x, everything from hi on is &gt;= x\". The original \u003ccode>hi = len(a) - 1\u003c/code> excluded the past-the-end answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to make a random tester find and shrink a failing input, and how to fix the bug by restoring the invariant it broke.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","p:27":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a mutable integer list and a target value, remove all occurrences of the target in place. Return the number of retained elements. Only the prefix of that length matters after the call; its order may be arbitrary, and anything beyond it is irrelevant. For \u003ccode>[3, 1, 3, 2]\u003c/code> with target 3, the returned length is 2 and a valid prefix is \u003ccode>[1, 2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice list has at most 100 elements and may be empty. It is not necessarily sorted, and any number of copies of the target may occur. The output must reside in the original list, so constructing a filtered replacement without writing it back is not enough.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove every copy of one value in place, return how many remain, and the order of what remains is free.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use one index to read every item and another to mark where the next item you keep should go. What is true of the part before the second index?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Region invariants for in-place work\u003c/p>\u003cp>Read/write pointers: a[:w] always holds the non-val items of a[:r]; copy a[r] to a[w] when it is not val. Since w &lt;= r, nothing unread is overwritten, and w is the answer when r reaches the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Collect all non-target elements in a separate list and copy them into the original prefix. This is O(n) time and O(n) extra space. Alternatively, deleting matches one by one can shift the remaining suffix repeatedly, making the worst case O(n^2).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read each position once and write each retained value to the next free prefix slot. Before reading index \u003ccode>read\u003c/code>, the prefix \u003ccode>nums[:write]\u003c/code> consists exactly of the non-target values in the original processed prefix, in their original order. Since \u003ccode>write &lt;= read\u003c/code>, a write never destroys an unread value. Skipping a target or appending a non-target preserves the invariant, and the final write count is the required length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [3, 1, 3, 2], target 3; write = 0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Skip 3; copy 1 to index 0 and advance write to 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Skip the next 3; copy 2 to index 1 and advance write to 2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return {{2}}; the meaningful prefix is [1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_element(nums, target):\n    write = 0\n    for read in range(len(nums)):\n        if nums[read] != target:\n            nums[write] = nums[read]\n            write += 1\n    return write\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One scan takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. This stable implementation preserves the relative order of retained elements even though the problem does not require that property.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check only the returned prefix, not the unspecified suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An all-target input returns zero; an empty input also returns zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Advance the write pointer only when keeping an item.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reassigning \u003ccode>nums\u003c/code> locally does not modify the caller's list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Allow unstable order and swap unwanted elements with the unprocessed end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Remove values satisfying a predicate instead of equality with one target.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:26":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compress a sorted integer list in place so its leading portion contains each distinct value once, in sorted order. Return the length of that prefix. For \u003ccode>[1, 1, 2, 2, 4]\u003c/code>, the returned length is 3 and the prefix is \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4]\u003c/code>. The remaining positions are not part of the answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice input contains between 1 and 30,000 integers in nondecreasing order. Equal values are therefore contiguous, so detecting the next distinct value needs only the last retained value. The function also supports an empty list even though the original minimum length is one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The array is sorted and each value must appear once at the front, in place.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In a sorted array, equal values sit next to each other. Which already-kept value must a new item differ from?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Region invariants for in-place work\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a write index w with the invariant \"a[:w] holds the distinct values of a[:r]\"; a[r] is new exactly when it differs from the last kept value a[w - 1], so copy it to a[w] and advance. Comparing with a[w] instead is the classic bug.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a separate list by checking whether each value has already appeared in that list, then copy it into the original prefix. Linear membership checks may take O(n^2) time and the list uses O(n) space. A set makes membership faster but still requires extra storage and does not by itself establish sorted order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At the start of each iteration, the prefix before \u003ccode>write\u003c/code> contains exactly the distinct values from the original processed part, in order. With sorted input, a new value either equals the last kept value or is greater than it; no earlier distinct value can reappear later. Copy a new value to \u003ccode>write\u003c/code> and advance it. The write index never exceeds the read index, so the scan does not corrupt unread data. At termination the invariant gives the required prefix and count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [1, 1, 2, 2, 4]; write = 0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Keep first 1; skip the second 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Keep first 2, skip the second 2, then keep 4\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return {{3}} and keep [1, 2, 4] in the prefix\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_duplicates(nums):\n    write = 0\n    for read in range(len(nums)):\n        if write == 0 or nums[read] != nums[write - 1]:\n            nums[write] = nums[read]\n            write += 1\n    return write\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each input position is read once, giving O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. The caller's list retains its original allocated length; the returned count identifies its meaningful prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare against \u003ccode>nums[write - 1]\u003c/code>, the last kept value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not inspect that position before any value has been kept.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This algorithm requires sorted input; separated duplicates on unsorted input are not removed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>All equal values produce a length of one, not the original length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Permit at most two copies of each value in the output prefix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the counts of each distinct value instead of modifying the list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:58":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a string of words separated by ordinary spaces, return the length of its last word. Leading, repeated and trailing spaces may occur. For \u003ccode>\"  red blue  \"\u003c/code>, the answer is 4. A word is a maximal run of non-space characters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice input has length at most 10,000, contains English letters and spaces, and has at least one word. The function below additionally returns zero for an empty or all-space string. It treats only the ordinary space character as a separator, matching the stated input domain.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The string may end with spaces, and only the last run of letters matters.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Where does the last word end, and where does it start? Think about walking from the right end of the string.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Edge cases and systematic debugging\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan from the end: skip trailing spaces, then count letters until a space or the start. The edge cases (trailing spaces, a single word, leading spaces) are handled by the two loops; s.split()[-1] is an O(n)-space oracle for tests.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Split the string on spaces, discard empty pieces and return the length of the last remaining piece. This takes O(n) time and O(n) extra space for the pieces. If the extended empty-input contract is desired, first check whether any pieces exist.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Nothing after the last word affects its length. Scan backwards, first discarding trailing spaces, then counting consecutive non-space characters. When the second scan stops, either the beginning of the string has been reached or the previous character is a separator. Thus exactly the last maximal word has been counted. The index decreases in both loops, ensuring termination.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input \"  red blue  \": begin at the final space\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Skip both trailing spaces; the index now points at e\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Count e, u, l, b moving left; stop at the space\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return {{4}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def last_word_length(s):\n    i = len(s) - 1\n    while i &gt;= 0 and s[i] == \" \":\n        i -= 1\n    length = 0\n    while i &gt;= 0 and s[i] != \" \":\n        length += 1\n        i -= 1\n    return length\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most n characters are inspected, so worst-case time is O(n), with O(1) auxiliary space. If the last word and trailing spaces are short, the scan need not visit the earlier part of the string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Skip trailing spaces before counting, or the result may incorrectly be zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test \u003ccode>i &gt;= 0\u003c/code> before indexing: Python accepts negative indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A one-character word and a word reaching index zero need no special branch.\u003c/li>\u003cli>General Unicode whitespace is a different contract from a literal space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the last word itself, using the two discovered boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat all Unicode whitespace as separators using a clearly specified predicate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:66":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An array stores a nonnegative integer as decimal digits in most-significant-first order. Add one and return the resulting digit list. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 9]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[1, 3, 0]\u003c/code>; for \u003ccode>[9, 9]\u003c/code>, it is \u003ccode>[1, 0, 0]\u003c/code>. This implementation may mutate the input list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice input contains 1 to 100 digits, each from 0 to 9, with no leading zero except for the single digit zero. A carry may propagate through every digit, requiring one additional leading digit. The nonempty valid-digit precondition is assumed rather than checked.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A number is stored as a list of decimal digits and you add one to it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Do it as you would on paper from the rightmost digit. Which input makes the result longer than the input?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Edge cases and systematic debugging\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk from the right, turning 9s into 0s while the carry continues; the first digit below 9 is incremented and you are done. If every digit was 9, the answer is a 1 followed by all zeros, the edge case most solutions forget.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space apart from the possible new list\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Convert the full digit sequence to an integer, add one, and convert the result back to decimal digits. This is straightforward for small inputs but uses O(n) extra space for conversion and may exceed integer limits in fixed-width languages. It is an independent small-input oracle for the digit algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Adding one starts with carry one at the least significant digit. A nine plus that carry becomes zero and still carries one; a digit below nine absorbs the carry and increases by one. Scan from right to left, maintaining that all already visited positions contain the correct final zeros and the carry still needs to be added to the unvisited prefix. If the scan runs past the front, every digit was nine, so prepend a one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [1, 2, 9]; start at the last digit with carry 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">9 becomes 0, and the carry continues\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">2 absorbs the carry and becomes 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return {{[1, 3, 0]}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def plus_one(digits):\n    for i in range(len(digits) - 1, -1, -1):\n        if digits[i] &lt; 9:\n            digits[i] += 1\n            return digits\n        digits[i] = 0\n    return [1] + digits\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most n digits are visited: O(n) time. Auxiliary space is O(1) when a digit absorbs the carry; the all-nine case creates an O(n) result list. That case also leaves the original list mutated to zeros, so callers should use the returned list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Continue the carry after changing a nine to zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stop immediately after incrementing a smaller digit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>All nines require a new leading one; \u003ccode>[0]\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning only the original object cannot express the extra digit without resizing it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add an arbitrary single decimal digit, tracking its carry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalize to a base b with digits from 0 to b - 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1299":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Replace each entry of a mutable integer list with the maximum of the entries originally to its right. Replace the final entry with -1. Return the modified list. For \u003ccode>[4, 8, 2, 5]\u003c/code>, the output is \u003ccode>[8, 5, 5, -1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice list has 1 to 10,000 positive integers, each at most 100,000. Positivity matters: -1 is smaller than every real element and can represent the empty suffix at the last position. The function also accepts an empty list and returns it unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each position must be replaced by the largest value strictly to its right, and the last becomes -1.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The brute force takes a maximum over every suffix. What could you carry from one position to the next if you walked in the other direction?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Loop invariants and variants\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk right to left with best = max of everything already passed (initially -1); write best into position i, then update best with the old a[i]. The invariant \"best = max(a[i + 1:])\" makes each answer O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each position, scan the original suffix to its right for a maximum, using -1 for the empty suffix. Preserve a copy or create a separate output so earlier mutations cannot affect later scans. The nested scans take O(n^2) time and the separate storage takes O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Moving right to left, maintain the maximum of the original elements strictly to the right of the current position. This is initially the empty-suffix sentinel -1. Save the current original value before overwriting it with the suffix maximum, then incorporate that saved value into the maximum for the next position. The invariant is true before the first iteration, maintained by the update, and supplies the required answer at every write.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [4, 8, 2, 5]; best = -1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write -1 at the last index, then update best to 5\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write 5 over 2, then 5 over 8; update best to 8\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write 8 over 4: result {{[8, 5, 5, -1]}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def replace_with_right_max(nums):\n    best = -1\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1, -1, -1):\n        old = nums[i]\n        nums[i] = best\n        best = max(best, old)\n    return nums\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One backwards pass uses O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. No suffix arrays are required. If the input can contain values below -1, the sentinel needs separate treatment instead of participating in the maximum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Update best using the saved original value, not the replacement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write before updating best; otherwise the current value wrongly participates in its own suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single-element input becomes \u003ccode>[-1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The positive-input assumption is essential to using -1 in \u003ccode>max\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute suffix maxima for arbitrary signed values using a separate empty-suffix state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace each value by the minimum strictly to its left.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:374":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A hidden integer is in the inclusive range 1 through n. A supplied function \u003ccode>guess(x)\u003c/code> returns -1 if the hidden number is lower than x, 1 if it is higher, and 0 if x is correct. Find the hidden number while making as few calls as logarithmic search needs. For n = 10 and a hidden value of 7, a search can find it without trying every number.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The practice range has \u003ccode>1 &lt;= n &lt;= 2^31 - 1\u003c/code>, so linear guessing is too slow. The response is consistent with one fixed hidden number, and the callback follows the sign convention above. The implementation uses a half-open interval and assumes the stated preconditions; the search may return the last remaining candidate without calling the callback on it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You can only ask higher, lower or correct about a hidden number in 1..n, with n up to 2^31 - 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep the range of numbers that could still be the answer. What should each question do to that range, and how do you write it so it always shrinks?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Loop invariants and variants\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the candidates in a half-open range [lo, hi), starting at [1, n + 1); guess mid = (lo + hi) // 2 and set hi = mid if the pick is lower, lo = mid + 1 if higher. The range always shrinks, so at most n.bit_length() guesses (31) are needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(log n) guesses, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Call \u003ccode>guess\u003c/code> on 1, 2, and so on until the response is zero. In the worst case this uses n calls and O(1) space. It is an uncomplicated oracle for small simulated ranges but not for the largest allowed n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a candidate interval \u003ccode>[lo, hi)\u003c/code> containing the hidden number. Initially it is \u003ccode>[1, n + 1)\u003c/code>. Query its midpoint. If the hidden number is lower, discard the midpoint and everything above it; if higher, discard the midpoint and everything below it. A correct response can be returned immediately. The interval stays nonempty and contains the target; each nonzero response strictly decreases its size. When one candidate remains, it must be the hidden number.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 10, hidden = 7: candidates [1, 11), midpoint 6\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Response 1 means higher; candidates become [7, 11)\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Midpoint 9 gets response -1; candidates become [7, 9)\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Midpoint 8 gets response -1; only 7 remains, return {{7}}\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def guess_number(n, guess):\n    lo, hi = 1, n + 1\n    while hi - lo &gt; 1:\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n        response = guess(mid)\n        if response == 0:\n            return mid\n        if response &lt; 0:\n            hi = mid\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n    return lo\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each query removes at least about half the candidates, giving O(log n) callback calls and O(1) auxiliary space. The half-open upper endpoint n + 1 needs a sufficiently wide integer type in fixed-width languages; Python integers do not overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check the callback's sign convention; the meaning of lower and higher must not be reversed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A midpoint ruled out by a nonzero response must not remain in the interval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For n = 1 the initial interval already contains only the answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Inconsistent callback answers violate the contract and are not detected here.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Find the first true position of a monotone Boolean predicate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use an inclusive interval and prove its endpoint updates separately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
